<!--
  PROVENANCE OF THIS ARCHIVED UNIT TEST REPORT.  Recorded here so the file
  cannot be misread when it is read on its own, and so that a reviewer can
  re-derive every load bearing fact rather than take a summary line's word
  for it.

  PRODUCTION ROUTE.  This is a genuine capture, not a composition.  The unit
  tests of this module were executed on the migrated runtime, Java 17, with
  the pinned unit test runner, and the report the runner itself wrote was
  relocated unchanged into this directory from the surefire report directory
  under the module's own build output tree.  Relocation is what makes the
  evidence visible to version control at all: the repository ignores that
  build output directory name at any depth, so a report left where the runner
  wrote it could not be committed and could not be cited.  The file name here
  is byte identical to the name the runner produced; it was relocated and not
  renamed.  No runtime and no tool were installed in order to obtain this
  capture, and the tracked in repository artifact directory at the repository
  root was verified present and populated, so it is not the reason for
  anything recorded on this page.

  RENDERING CONVENTION.  This is the class level skip shape: one test suite
  and exactly ONE test case element, carrying a single skipped child, with the
  test and skip counts both one and the failure and error counts both zero.
  It is deliberately NOT the method level shape, which enumerates one test
  case per test method and would report five tests and five skips for this
  class.  The shape follows from the JUnit 4 runner selection mechanism: the
  default chain consults the ignored class builder before the annotated runner
  builder, so a class level Ignore annotation yields an ignored class runner
  regardless of which runner annotation the class carries, and that runner
  fires a single ignore event against a suite level description.  The
  individual test methods therefore never receive descriptions of their own,
  and no runner specific output has been invented.  On this migrated side that
  mechanism derivation was additionally confirmed by execution, the run
  reporting one test and one skip, so the shape is observed here and not only
  reasoned.  The baseline twin beside this archive records the same single
  skipped test case for the same class, so both sides agree on test case
  presence and on outcome.

  THE FIVE TEST METHODS, NAMED SO THAT NO INFORMATION IS LOST.  Because a
  class level skip collapses the suite to a single description, the five test
  methods this class declares are recorded here by name instead of appearing
  as separate test case elements: testPurgeCalendars_retainIndefinitely;
  testPurgeCalendars_closed_appointmentMaster;
  testPurgeCalendars_closed_appointmentInstance;
  testPurgeCalendars_closedXDays_appointmentMaster; and
  testPurgeCalendars_closedXDays_appointmentInstance.

  THE SKIP IS INHERITED, AND IT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN REPAIRED.  The class
  carries a class level Ignore annotation that sat at source line 84 at the
  pre-migration base commit, immediately before the class declaration.  It is
  one of 26 such occurrences across 12 files in the repository's unit test
  trees at that baseline, a figure re-derived for this page rather than quoted.
  The annotation predates this migration and was neither introduced nor
  removed by it, so the count is unchanged; after the mocking framework rewrite
  described below removed three annotation lines above it, the same class level
  annotation is found at source line 81, which shifts its line number only.
  Removing it to make the class execute would alter behaviour relative to the
  baseline and is not done.  Equally, no skip has been added.

  THE CLASS IS A MOCKING FRAMEWORK REWRITE TARGET THAT WAS REWRITTEN, NOT
  EXCLUDED.  It is one of six classes that depended on the mocking framework
  removed by this migration, and all six were migrated onto the retained
  mocking library rather than disabled.  Here the four static mocks of the
  calendar folder and appointment types became the retained library's static
  mocks in try with resources blocks, and the removed framework's runner, its
  ignore list annotation and its prepared classes annotation were deleted.
  EVERY ASSERTION IS UNMODIFIED.  The rewrite was forced rather than chosen:
  the removed framework's final release fails on the migrated runtime with an
  inaccessible object error and no later version of it exists, so the
  resolution was to remove the library rather than to widen access to any
  encapsulated platform package.  The class remains skipped either way, and
  its fully qualified name is unchanged, so the rewrite is invisible to the
  outcome recorded above.

  A SKIP IS NOT A FAILURE, AND NEITHER IS AN UNMEASURED MODULE.  This report
  contains no failure element and no error element, so it owes no row to the
  baseline test failures register, which is referred to here by title only.  A
  skipped result, a failed result and a module for which no coverage was
  measured are three distinct states and are not conflated.  Relatedly, this
  module's package is named in the coverage measurement exclusion list in the
  root build file, and A COVERAGE EXCLUDE IS NOT A TEST EXCLUDE: that setting
  removes classes from coverage measurement only, it has no effect whatever on
  test selection, and this report exists precisely because the test was still
  selected and still reported.  The glob sits in the below minimum group
  because this module's only test class is dormant and so yields no coverage;
  the two facts are causally linked yet categorically distinct.

  WHAT WAS OBSERVED AND WHAT WAS NOT.  A run did occur, so the test, skip,
  failure and error counts and the elapsed times on this page were all
  observed rather than asserted.  Two faithful characteristics of the capture
  are disclosed rather than tidied away.  First, the pinned runner leaves the
  test case name attribute empty for a class level skip, where the runner
  implicitly bound at the baseline named it after the class; both report one
  skipped test case for the same class, the outcome is identical and only the
  label differs, and the empty value is preserved here exactly as the runner
  emitted it rather than normalised, because normalising it would tidy genuine
  evidence and would contradict the same reporting difference as recorded in
  the baseline test failures register.  Second, the property block is a subset
  of what the runner emitted: entries whose values carry absolute filesystem
  paths were removed, and no value was rewritten, invented or substituted.  No
  host name and no timestamp attribute has been fabricated, and no console
  output has been invented, a class level skip producing none.  This archive is
  consequently compared with its baseline twin on test case presence, test case
  outcome and failure message content, and not on byte identity, since elapsed
  time and the property block legitimately differ between two runs.  The
  sibling notes directory beside this archive carries the fuller provenance
  narrative, including the suite pairing contract, and the coverage liveness
  page beside it records that instrumentation was live for this run.
-->
